---
import FutureTechMissionFrame from '../../../../components/FutureTechMissionFrame.astro';
---
<FutureTechMissionFrame
  missionNumber="4"
  title="Robot Rookie"
  tagline="Make code control real robot parts."
  question="How can code control real robot parts to create light and movement?"
  success="I can connect a Hummingbird LED and position servo, control them with MakeCode, combine light and movement into a simple robotic action, and explain how my code matches the hardware ports."
  nextTitle="Mission 5 — Sense → Think → Act"
  nextHref="../mission-5-sense-think-act/"
>
  <div class="robot-flow" aria-label="Mission robotics flow">
    <span>CODE</span><b>→</b><span>PORT</span><b>→</b><span>ROBOT OUTPUT</span>
  </div>
  <div class="output-branches" aria-label="Robot output types">
    <span><b>LED PORT 1</b><small>LIGHT</small></span>
    <span><b>SERVO PORT 1</b><small>MOTION</small></span>
  </div>
  <p class="flow-note">Your code controls the part connected to the matching Hummingbird port.</p>

  <section id="stage-1" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">01</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">ROBOT HARDWARE</p><h2 class="stage-title">Set Up</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Today your code leaves the micro:bit and controls real robot parts. Set up the Hummingbird carefully before you turn on its power.</p>

    <div class="check-list"><span>☐ Computer</span><span>☐ micro:bit V2</span><span>☐ USB data cable</span><span>☐ Hummingbird Bit Controller</span><span>☐ Battery pack / approved power supply</span><span>☐ Single-color LED</span><span>☐ Position servo + horn</span><span>☐ Terminal tool if your station uses one</span></div>

    <p class="success"><b>Individual mission:</b> build, test, and explain your own robot outputs. Share a hardware station only if controller quantities require it.</p>

    <div class="station-card"><strong>YOUR HUMMINGBIRD STATION</strong><span>HB-01 · HB-02 · HB-03...</span><p>Stay with your assigned station while testing. Keep your own MakeCode project even if another student uses the hardware later.</p></div>

    <p class="mini-label">Hummingbird Bit — connect only what you need</p>
    <div class="hardware-diagram" role="img" aria-label="Simplified Hummingbird Bit diagram showing the micro:bit connection, LED Port 1, Servo Port 1, power connector, and status lights. LED wiring is colored wire to positive and black wire to negative. Servo wiring is white to signal, red to positive, and black to negative.">
      <div class="callout led-callout"><span>LIGHT</span><strong>LED PORT 1</strong><p>colored wire → <b>+</b><br/>black wire → <b>–</b></p></div>
      <div class="hb-board">
        <div class="microbit-slot"><small>MICRO:BIT</small><strong>V2</strong></div>
        <div class="board-label">HUMMINGBIRD BIT</div>
        <div class="board-ports"><span class="port led-port"><b>LED 1</b><small>+ &nbsp; –</small></span><span class="port servo-port"><b>SERVO 1</b><small>S &nbsp; + &nbsp; –</small></span></div>
        <div class="board-bottom"><span>● STATUS</span><span>POWER ⏻</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout servo-callout"><span>MOTION</span><strong>SERVO PORT 1</strong><p>white → <b>S</b><br/>red → <b>+</b><br/>black → <b>–</b></p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hardware-caption-grid">
      <span><b>MICRO:BIT</b><small>Insert correctly. Never force it.</small></span>
      <span><b>POWER</b><small>OFF while connecting or changing parts. ON only when testing.</small></span>
      <span><b>STATUS</b><small>The Hummingbird needs its own power before the servo can move.</small></span>
    </div>

    <div class="warning safety-card"><strong>CONNECT WITH POWER OFF</strong><ol><li><b>Power OFF first.</b></li><li>Insert the micro:bit correctly. Do not force it.</li><li><b>LED Port 1:</b> colored wire → <b>+</b> · black wire → <b>–</b>.</li><li><b>Servo Port 1:</b> white → <b>S</b> · red → <b>+</b> · black → <b>–</b>.</li><li>Compare both connections to the diagram <b>before</b> turning power on.</li><li>Turn Hummingbird power ON only when you are ready to test.</li><li>Before changing, removing, or reconnecting any robot part, <b>turn power OFF again</b>.</li></ol></div>
    <p class="warning"><b>SERVO MOVES:</b> keep fingers, hair, sleeves, wires, and loose materials away from the servo horn while power is on.</p>

    <div class="power-cue"><span><b>CONNECT / CHANGE</b><small>POWER OFF</small></span><i>↔</i><span><b>TEST</b><small>POWER ON</small></span></div>

    <p class="mini-label">Code → Port → Output</p>
    <div class="port-map" aria-label="Code to port to output mapping">
      <article><span>MAKECODE</span><strong>Hummingbird LED · port 1</strong><b>→</b><span>HARDWARE</span><strong>LED PORT 1</strong><b>→</b><span>OUTPUT</span><strong>LIGHT</strong></article>
      <article><span>MAKECODE</span><strong>Hummingbird Position Servo · port 1</strong><b>→</b><span>HARDWARE</span><strong>SERVO PORT 1</strong><b>→</b><span>OUTPUT</span><strong>MOTION</strong></article>
    </div>
    <p class="concept">The number in your code must match the number on the controller.</p>

    <div class="quick-check"><strong>60-SECOND HARDWARE CHECK</strong><span>Which part makes light?</span><span>Which part makes motion?</span><span>Which port does each part use?</span></div>
    <p class="success">✓ <b>You are ready when:</b> the LED is in LED Port 1, the position servo is in Servo Port 1, both wire orders match the diagram, power is OFF while you check the connections, and you can explain what a port does.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-2" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">02</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">FIRST ROBOT OUTPUT</p><h2 class="stage-title">First Code</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Start with the easiest robot output: light. Make the external LED blink before you add any motion.</p>

    <div class="step-grid">
      <article class="step-card"><strong>1 — Open MakeCode</strong><p>Open <a class="external" href="https://makecode.microbit.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MakeCode for micro:bit ↗</a>. Create <code>FirstName_L1_M4</code>.</p></article>
      <article class="step-card"><strong>2 — Add Hummingbird</strong><p>Add the Hummingbird extension so the <b>Hummingbird</b> category appears in the toolbox.</p></article>
      <article class="step-card"><strong>3 — Start Hummingbird</strong><p>Put <code>Start Hummingbird</code> inside <code>on start</code>.</p></article>
      <article class="step-card"><strong>4 — Blink LED 1</strong><p>Use the Hummingbird <code>Hummingbird LED</code> block for port 1, then download to the real hardware.</p></article>
    </div>

    <div class="block-finder"><span><b>Hummingbird</b> → Start Hummingbird</span><span><b>Hummingbird</b> → Hummingbird LED</span><span><b>Basic</b> → pause</span></div>

    <aside class="code-model" aria-label="Code model 1 of 2 showing Start Hummingbird in on start and LED port 1 blinking in forever">
      <div class="code-model-head"><span>CODE MODEL 1 OF 2</span><strong>Start Hummingbird + blink LED 1</strong></div>
      <div class="code-stack">
        <div class="mk-block event">on start</div>
        <div class="mk-block humming indent">Start Hummingbird</div>
        <div class="mk-gap"></div>
        <div class="mk-block loops">forever</div>
        <div class="mk-block humming indent">Hummingbird LED <span class="value emphasis">1</span> <span class="value">100%</span></div>
        <div class="mk-block basic indent">pause <span class="value">500 ms</span></div>
        <div class="mk-block humming indent">Hummingbird LED <span class="value emphasis">1</span> <span class="value">0%</span></div>
        <div class="mk-block basic indent">pause <span class="value">500 ms</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="model-map"><b>LED 1 in code</b><span>→</span><b>LED Port 1 on board</b></div>
      <p class="code-model-note"><b>Brightness 100% = full brightness.</b> Brightness 0% = off.</p>
    </aside>

    <p class="concept"><b>PORT MATCH:</b> LED 1 in your code controls the LED connected to LED Port 1.</p>
    <p class="success">✓ The external LED blinks on and off repeatedly.</p>
    <p class="warning"><b>LED not lighting?</b> Check colored wire → + · black wire → – · LED Port 1 · Start Hummingbird · newest download.</p>

    <div class="change-card"><strong>CHANGE ONE THING</strong><span>Change the blink speed</span><b>OR</b><span>Change brightness from 100% to another value</span></div>
    <p class="success">✓ Your LED still works after your change.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-3" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">03</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">CODE → MOTION</p><h2 class="stage-title">Make It React</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Now add controlled motion. Centre the position servo safely before you attach or align anything to it.</p>

    <div class="warning centering-card"><strong>CENTER THE SERVO FIRST</strong><div class="centering-cue"><span>Hummingbird</span><b>→</b><span>Hummingbird Position Servo</span><b>→</b><span>Servo 1</span><b>→</b><span>90°</span></div><ol><li>Confirm the servo is still connected to <b>Servo Port 1</b>.</li><li>With power OFF, keep the horn / moving piece unattached or loose.</li><li>Add one <b>Hummingbird Position Servo</b> command set to <b>Servo 1 → 90°</b>.</li><li>Turn power ON, download, and let the servo move to centre.</li><li>Turn power OFF again.</li><li>Only then attach or align the servo horn / moving piece at its centred position.</li></ol></div>

    <div class="angle-cue" aria-label="Servo angle guide"><span><b>30°</b><small>one side</small></span><i>←</i><span class="center"><b>90°</b><small>centre</small></span><i>→</i><span><b>150°</b><small>other side</small></span></div>

    <div class="block-finder"><span><b>Hummingbird</b> → Hummingbird Position Servo</span><span><b>Input</b> → on button A pressed</span><span><b>Input</b> → on button B pressed</span><span><b>Hummingbird</b> → Hummingbird LED</span></div>

    <aside class="code-model" aria-label="Code model 2 of 2 showing Button A and Button B controlling position servo 1 at two angles">
      <div class="code-model-head"><span>CODE MODEL 2 OF 2</span><strong>Buttons control servo positions</strong></div>
      <div class="code-columns">
        <div class="code-stack"><div class="mk-block input">on button <span class="value">A</span> pressed</div><div class="mk-block humming indent">Hummingbird Position Servo <span class="value emphasis">1</span> <span class="value">30°</span></div></div>
        <div class="code-stack"><div class="mk-block input">on button <span class="value">B</span> pressed</div><div class="mk-block humming indent">Hummingbird Position Servo <span class="value emphasis">1</span> <span class="value">150°</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="model-map"><b>Servo 1 in code</b><span>→</span><b>Servo Port 1 on board</b></div>
      <p class="code-model-note">A position servo moves to the angle you choose and stays there until another command changes it.</p>
    </aside>

    <p class="success">✓ Button A moves the servo to one position. Button B moves it to a clearly different position.</p>

    <div class="route"><span>BUTTON</span><span>SERVO ANGLE</span><span>LED BRIGHTNESS</span></div>
    <p class="intro">Now add the LED to one or both button events.</p>
    <div class="step-grid">
      <article class="step-card"><strong>STATE A IDEA</strong><p>Servo moves · LED turns on.</p></article>
      <article class="step-card"><strong>STATE B IDEA</strong><p>Servo moves somewhere different · LED turns off.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="mini-note">These are examples, not a finished Robot Signal.</p>
    <p class="success">✓ One button changes both motion and light, and you can point to the matching Port 1 blocks in your code.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-4" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">04</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">YOUR ROBOT</p><h2 class="stage-title">Build It — Robot Signal</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Build a simple robotic signal, character, or device that uses <b>one light and one moving part</b> to communicate two different states.</p>

    <div class="planning-grid"><article><strong>STATE A</strong><p>What should the robot show or do?</p></article><article><strong>STATE B</strong><p>What should change?</p></article><article><strong>MEANING</strong><p>What are the light and movement communicating?</p></article></div>

    <p class="mini-label">Need a theme?</p>
    <div class="idea-grid"><span>🚧 <b>Crossing Gate</b></span><span>🦾 <b>Robot Arm</b></span><span>⚠ <b>Warning Signal</b></span><span>🐾 <b>Creature Head / Tail</b></span><span>🚪 <b>Mini Door</b></span><span>🚩 <b>Signal Flag</b></span></div>

    <div class="check-list"><span>☐ Hummingbird starts correctly in code</span><span>☐ LED uses <b>LED Port 1</b></span><span>☐ Position servo uses <b>Servo Port 1</b></span><span>☐ Button A creates one clear robot state</span><span>☐ Button B creates a different robot state</span><span>☐ At least one state changes the LED</span><span>☐ Both states use a meaningful servo position</span><span>☐ Servo stays within safe, useful angles</span><span>☐ Light + motion match the intended meaning</span><span>☐ I made at least one meaningful design choice</span><span>☐ Robot works after a fresh download</span><span>☐ I can explain <b>code → port → output</b></span></div>

    <p class="concept">You decide the physical theme, servo angle A, servo angle B, LED behavior, and what each state means.</p>
    <p class="success">✓ <b>Build It is ready when:</b> Button A and Button B create two clear physical states using both light and motion, and you can explain why you chose those states.</p>

    <div class="try4"><strong>STUCK? TRY 4</strong><p><b>1 CHECK</b> — power → wire order → port number → code block/value → servo angle. <b>2 SIMULATE</b> — test the micro:bit button logic on screen. <b>3 DOWNLOAD</b> — send the newest program. <b>4 PARTNER</b> — briefly explain expected vs actual to a nearby student.</p><p>Still stuck? Ask for help and say what you already tried.</p></div>
    <p class="warning"><b>HARDWARE CHECK:</b> A working simulator does not prove the Hummingbird wiring or power is correct.</p>
    <p class="station-fault">If a component, cable, controller, or power pack still appears faulty after TRY 4, report the <b>HB station number</b> instead of moving parts between stations.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-5" class="stage">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">05</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">OPTIONAL CHALLENGE</p><h2 class="stage-title">Level It Up</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Only after Robot Signal works. Choose <b>one</b> while you wait for your checkpoint.</p>
    <div class="level-grid">
      <article class="level-card"><strong>⭐ Brightness Designer</strong><p>Use a brightness value between 1 and 99. Decide whether it improves your signal.</p></article>
      <article class="level-card"><strong>⭐ Three Positions</strong><p>Use A, B, and A+B to move the position servo to three different angles.</p></article>
      <article class="level-card"><strong>⭐⭐ Motion Sequence</strong><p>Move the servo through three positions with pauses: <b>30° → 90° → 150°</b>.</p></article>
      <article class="level-card"><strong>⭐⭐ Tri-Color Upgrade</strong><p>Replace or add one tri-color LED and use one simple color. This skill is not part of the checkpoint.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="warning"><b>MISSION 4 LIMIT:</b> no sensors, autonomous reactions, rotation servos, radio, or multiple motors yet.</p>
    <p class="success">When every Build It box is checked, add your name to <b>Ready-for-Check</b>. Keep Leveling Up until you are called.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stage-6" class="stage checkpoint">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">06</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">SHOW WHAT YOU KNOW</p><h2 class="stage-title">Checkpoint</h2></div></div>
    <p class="intro">Bring your robot and open your code when you are called. The checkpoint is individual.</p>
    <div class="step-grid">
      <article class="step-card"><strong>Check 1 — Trace the System</strong><p>Demonstrate one state. Trace <b>LED command → LED Port 1 → LED</b> and <b>servo command → Servo Port 1 → servo</b>.</p></article>
      <article class="step-card"><strong>Check 2 — Explain the Outputs</strong><p>Explain brightness, servo angle, why the code port must match the physical port, why Hummingbird power is needed for physical testing, and which part is the input.</p></article>
      <article class="step-card"><strong>Check 3 — Change It</strong><p>Change one brightness, servo angle, button mapping, or pause. Then <b>predict → change → download → test → explain</b>.</p></article>
      <article class="step-card"><strong>After Your Check</strong><p><b>MISSION COMPLETE</b> · <b>RETRY ONE SKILL</b> · <b>SUPPORT ROUTE</b></p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stage passport">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">✓</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">SKILL PASSPORT</p><h2 class="stage-title">Mission 4 Skills</h2></div></div>
    <div class="check-list"><span>☐ I can safely connect a Hummingbird robot output.</span><span>☐ I can match a MakeCode port number to a physical Hummingbird port.</span><span>☐ I can control the brightness of an external LED.</span><span>☐ I can control the angle of a position servo.</span><span>☐ I can use a micro:bit input to control robot outputs.</span><span>☐ I can change, download, test, and explain my robot independently.</span></div>
  </section>

  <section class="stage reset">
    <div class="stage-head"><span class="stage-number">↺</span><div><p class="stage-kicker">LEAVE IT READY</p><h2 class="stage-title">Reset</h2></div></div>
    <div class="check-list"><span>☐ Project saved correctly</span><span>☐ Hummingbird power switched off</span><span>☐ micro:bit returned to numbered kit</span><span>☐ USB cable loosely coiled</span><span>☐ Single-color LED returned</span><span>☐ Position servo + horn returned</span><span>☐ Battery pack / power lead returned</span><span>☐ Terminal tool returned if used</span><span>☐ Hummingbird controller returned to its station</span><span>☐ Loose build materials cleared</span><span>☐ Faults / bent pins / loose wires reported</span><span>☐ Passport/progress updated</span></div>
    <p class="concept">Exit question: What is the difference between telling an LED what brightness to use and telling a servo what angle to use?</p>
  </section>
</FutureTechMissionFrame>

<style is:global>
